## 1. The one principle that keeps you safe

You are **not** deciding whether DDM saves 30%. You are **building the instrument that makes the claim checkable**, and reporting the current state honestly.

Say this out loud, in these words, to everyone you talk to:

> "I'm not here to decide whether DDM is worth it. Kylee asked me to establish the current effort baseline so any efficiency claim can be measured against something real. I need your help getting the baseline right."

This does three things at once: it defuses the Sunil/Sridhar history (you're not on a side), it protects you (you never author the 30%), and it makes people willing to give you honest numbers (you're measuring the work, not judging them).

**Repeat it whenever the conversation drifts toward "is DDM good."** Redirect to "what does the current work actually take."

